    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d167">

                <pb facs="0215" n="125" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav02/pg125"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/1690</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Russia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Francis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vologda</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-04-18"><hi rendition="#italic">April 18, 1918</hi></date>.
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received April 30, 8.58 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>109. Continuing my 107, 17th. Following just received from
                        Webster, Omsk, through Robins: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Leaving 18th for Moscow. Investigations, Krasnoyarsk, Omsk, give entirely
                            satisfactory results confirming impressions communicated previous
                            telegrams. No armed prisoners Krasnoyarsk, discipline strict. Omsk chief
                            center arming prisoners, total, including those sent against Semenov, 1, 100. Omsk provincial arming
                            prisoners confirmed. Guarantees obtained from All-Siberian Soviet. All
                            armed prisoners violently socialistic. Present conditions without menace
                            except against Central Empires or Japanese invasion.</p></quote></p>
                <p>Mail just received from Consul General enclosing telegrams from Macgowan, Jenkins, Thomson,
                        Nielsen. Do not accord with
                        Webster. Macgowan reported, 12th, had urged Webster
                    visit Omsk, but said Webster party likewise disposed in
                    this as in other matters to accept Bolshevik assurance that it was nothing.
                        Thomson, Omsk,<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d167-f1"/> April 5, reported thousand prisoners near there fully
                    equipped under German officers and suggested that arming prisoners might be with
                    German approval to oppose Japanese invasion. Jenkins, Chita, 13th, reports decree of local Soviet government
                    declaring prisoners free as other inhabitants. You see reports disagree.
                        Robins already cabled Davison, Thompson, that Webster investigation
                    “admirably done.”</p>
                <pb facs="0216" n="126" xml:id="pg126"/>
                <p>Petrograd press says Chinese press report two detachments of English and American
                    troops arrived Harbin. Is it true? Caldwell wires Chinese cruiser arrived at Vladivostok.</p>
                <p>American Consul, Petrograd, reports Viborg surrounded and rumored that few
                    Russian vessels sunk in Finnish ports to avoid German capture. See Robins’s cable Thompson through Davison.
                    Passengers [reference my] 108, Davison.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d167-f2"/></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Francis</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
